  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since March 2021, the Tonga Public Services Union (TPSA) has extended support and new services to workers in the informal economy through the creation of new unions. Five new unions were formed and affiliated to TPSA, namely: (i) Tonga National Agricultural Workers&#x2019; Association; (ii) Tonga National Animal Husbandry Workers&#x2019; Association; (iii) Tonga National Tapa-making Workers&#x2019; Association; (iv) Tonga National Weaving Workers&#x2019; Association; and (v) Tonga National Handicraft &amp; Small Tourism Workers&#x2019; Association were formed in June 2021.  These organizations represent an estimated 2,000 workers.

Furthermore, TPSA organized tradeshows for each of the newly formed associations to recover income losses due to COVID-19. The following are the results: 1. National Tapa making Workers&#x2019; Association &#x2013; 32 villages participated, 545 women engaged and Tonga Dollars 150,000 worth orders received and 2. National Weaving Workers Association &#x2013; 28 villages participating, 345 workers engaged, and orders received yet to be determined. 

Furthermore, TPSA supported, these organizations to secure benefits under the 2nd COVID-19 stimulus package in Tonga in May and June of 2021, extending greater outreach to workers in  the informal economy to recover better from the COVID-19 pandemic. ILO through the MPTF Project and RBTC provided the following support: 

1.	Initial discussions of reaching out to the informal economy and expanding trade union formation in the private sector were held with the Tonga Public Sector Association (TPSA) early 2020.
2.	Technical advice through webinars using resource persons from the Philippines trade union movement focusing on informal economy organizing methodologies and strategies in February  to May of 2021. 
3.	Support to conduct Rapid Assessment in Tonga to determine the effect and scale of support that trade unions needed to extend as new services to support worker sin the informal economy. 

The ILO continues to support the TPSA to solidify and strengthen the new organizations within the Tonga National Workers&#x2019; Organization.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.2.1. Number of national workers&#x2019; organizations with innovative strategies to attract new groups of workers and/or to improve their services</narrative>
    </title>
